---
import Container from './Container.astro'
import { site } from '../../content/site'
import logo from '../../../public/images/logo-ficho.svg?raw'
---
{/* Sticky, and it DOES have a bar — the two are one decision, which is why
    this comment has been rewritten twice. Figma's navbar (node 8921:2731's
    parent, 8920:2156) is a 1312x72 pill with a hairline border and a fill
    slightly lighter than the page. It was dropped along with the nav links
    when this header was reduced, and those were not the same decision: the
    links genuinely are absent from the design, the pill is not.

    On sticky: it was removed because "a sticky header without a background
    scrolls transparently over the content, which put 88px headings underneath
    the wordmark". Read the condition — *without a background*. That was true
    only while the pill was missing. With the pill back the header has an
    opaque surface of its own, content passing beneath it is covered, and the
    sole technical objection is gone. So sticky is restored deliberately, not
    reverted by accident, and the test that asserted the opposite was flipped
    on purpose.

    `sticky`, not `fixed`: sticky stays in flow, so nothing below shifts and no
    compensating padding has to be kept in sync with the header's height across
    breakpoints. `fixed` would buy nothing here and add exactly that coupling.

    `top-0`, not `top-4`, and the pt-8 does the offsetting instead. With
    `top-4` the header pins 16px down while its natural position is 0, so it
    sticks immediately and the pill sits 48px from the viewport top at rest —
    16px below where Figma draws it. `top-0` keeps the pill at the 32px pt-8
    gives it, both at rest and while pinned, so nothing moves on first scroll.

    The scrim is what makes that 32px gap safe, and its shape is deliberate.
    The pill is opaque, but the pt-8 above it belongs to this sticky element and
    was transparent, so content scrolled up through it — barely visible on the
    dark home page, glaring on the light case-study pages where large blue
    headings slid across the top edge.

    Two simpler fixes were tried in the browser and rejected on what they
    actually rendered. A flat opaque fill hid the content but covered
    body::before/::after for the header's full 104px, leaving a ~12-level
    brightness step at its bottom edge where the page's radial gradient resumed
    — the same class of seam the hero's fog mask exists to avoid. `backdrop-blur`
    alone left no seam but only smeared bright content, so a white work card
    still read as a pale smudge above the pill.

    This gradient does both: opaque through 40% (~42px) so the 0-32px gap is
    covered outright, then faded to nothing by 100%. The entire fade lives
    behind the opaque pill, so it is never seen, and alpha is already zero at
    the header's bottom edge — no step against the page. Do not flatten this to
    a solid colour.

    On the SectionIndicator: case-study pages fix a progress bar at
    `top: var(--header-h)`, which its own script sets from this header's
    measured height. That is 104px here (32 + 72) and it lands flush under the
    pill, so the two coordinate without either knowing the other's numbers.
    Changing pt-8 or the pill's height moves the indicator with it.

    Why the pill matters beyond fidelity: without it the header has no surface
    of its own and borrows whatever is behind it, so anything painted below —
    the hero's fog, for one — makes it read as a stray dark band. That is also
    what makes sticky safe now.

    Every value here is a token, not a hex. This header also renders on the
    light case-study pages, where BaseLayout remaps the colour tokens; a
    literal would have put a dark bar on #FAFAFA. Measured from Figma at full
    resolution the fill is #232324 -> #1A1B1D and the border is a gradient
    stroke #828287 -> #55555A. The gradient direction is reproduced through
    bg-elevated -> bg-secondary; the border is a flat token rather than a
    gradient ring, which is dimmer than Figma's stroke and the one deliberate
    approximation here. */}
<header class="sticky top-0 z-40 bg-[linear-gradient(to_bottom,var(--color-bg-primary)_0%,var(--color-bg-primary)_40%,transparent_100%)] pt-8">
  <Container>
    <div class="flex h-[72px] items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-full border border-border-hover bg-[linear-gradient(to_bottom,var(--color-bg-elevated),var(--color-bg-secondary))] pl-8 pr-3">
      {/* Inlined rather than an <img> so the wordmark's currentColor fill can
          inherit --color-text-primary. As an <img> it was Figma's hardcoded
          white, which vanished on the light case-study pages. The accessible
          name lives on the link, so the SVG itself is hidden — naming both
          would make the link announce itself twice. */}
      <a
        href="/"
        class="inline-flex h-8 items-center text-text-primary [&>svg]:h-8 [&>svg]:w-auto"
        aria-label={`${site.name} — home`}
      ><Fragment set:html={logo} /></a>

      <a
        href={`mailto:${site.email}`}
        class="inline-flex h-12 items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-border px-5 text-base font-semibold leading-none text-text-primary transition duration-300 hover:border-border-hover hover:bg-bg-elevated"
      >
        <span>Contact Us</span>
        <svg
          class="h-6 w-6"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
          <path d="M5 12h14" />
          <path d="m13 5 7 7-7 7" />
        </svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </Container>
</header>
